0. Definitions.

"The Program" means Flagpost, the CTF competition management platform
published at <https://github.com/tbcsec/flagpost> and licensed under
AGPL-3.0.

"Module Loader" means the Program's manifest-driven module loading
mechanism: the loader implemented at backend/plugins/loader.py and
invoked by the Program at startup (backend/main.py), which scans the
Program's module directory (backend/plugins/) for packages containing a
plugin.yaml manifest, resolves their declared dependencies, imports each
package, and calls its setup() entry point.  The mechanism is documented
in docs/ARCHITECTURE.md §11 ("Plugin System").  This exception uses the
Program documentation's own term for works loaded this way — "Modules";
where the mechanism's on-disk naming says "plugin" (the backend/plugins/
directory, the plugin.yaml manifest filename, the §11 heading), it names
the same mechanism, and the two terms are interchangeable here.

"Module API" means the extension interface the Module Loader provides to
a loaded package, namely:

  a) the module contract: a Python package placed in the module
     directory, containing a plugin.yaml manifest (declaring "id",
     "name", "version", and optionally "description", "provides", and
     "dependencies" on other Modules) and exposing a
     setup(app, event_bus, db_factory) entry point;

  b) the capabilities the Module Loader passes to setup(): the
     application object (through which a Module mounts its own HTTP
     routes), the Program's event bus (backend/utils/event_bus.py,
     through which a Module subscribes to and emits events in the
     Program's event vocabulary, backend/utils/event_catalog.py), and
     the database session factory; and

  c) run-time invocation of the Program: importing and calling the
     Program's Python interfaces from Module code executing in the same
     process, in the way the Module Loader makes possible for any loaded
     Module — provided the Module does not modify the Program (see
     Section 2).

"Module" means a separate and independent work that:

  a) extends or adds functionality to the Program solely through the
     Module API — that is, it is loaded by the Module Loader through the
     module contract, and interfaces with the Program by invoking it at
     run time as defined above;

  b) is developed and distributed separately from the Program — it is
     not part of the Program's own source tree (the repository
     identified above) and is not a derivative of the Modules that ship
     with the Program; and

  c) does not modify, patch, or replace the Program's own source code or
     its run-time behaviour, as set out in Section 2.

For clarity: installing a Module — copying the Module's own files into
the module directory (backend/plugins/) of an installed copy of the
Program, or including them at that path when building your own
deployment image — is how the Module Loader is designed to pick a Module
up, and does not by itself constitute modifying the Program or merging
the Module into the Program's source tree for the purposes of this
definition.

